Крещение "baptism":
Крестить "to baptize";
—> ProtoSlav. krьstъ "a cross": крест, Ukr. хрест, Est. rist, Hung. kereszt, Pol. chrzest;
—> Old High Ger. Krist;
—> Anc. Gr. Χριστός [Khristós] Messiah or Christ, an epithet of Jesus of Nazareth — literally “anointed one”: ☧, Chrīstus, Arm. Քրիստոս [Kʿristos], Ge. რისტე [krisṭe]. And names: Kirsten, Christopher, Kit, Christian;
—> From χρῑ́ω “to anoint; to rub, smear”: a calque from Heb. ⁧מָשִׁיחַ⁩ [mašīaḥ], Aramaic ⁧ܡܫܺܝܚܳܐ⁩ [m'šīḥāʿ]. See chrism "ointment", messiah.
—> PIE *gʰrey- “to smear”, *gʰer- “to rub”: grime, grimace, Sans. घृ [ghṛ] "to moisten", χρῶμα [khrôma] "skin, colour" — hence chromatin, chrome; great and Ger. groß (through “big in size, coarse, coarse grained”), груда [gruda] "a pile".

An answer: Croissant and crescendo are in the "creation" group with cereal and increase, крещение is in the "rubbing" group with chrome, grime and other greats.

