Славяне [slavjane]:
From proto-Sl *slověninъ “Slav”: Ch-Sl словѣне, Ukr слов'яни, Pol. Słowianie, Slovak Slovania, Gr Σκλαβηνοί, Lat Sklavīnî, Arab صقالبة‎ [ṣaqāliba];
3 main versions:
1) from PIE *ḱlew-, originally denoting "people who speak (the same language)" and understand each other, in contrast to němci, the Slavic word denoting "foreign people";
2) Vasmer: the suffix *-ě/aninъ seems to derive nouns only from toponyms (древляне, кыяне, бужане); hence this word is derived from a toponym, probably a hydronym: Rus Слуя, Polish Sława, Serbo-Cr Славница, and the most importantly Slavuta (the Dnipro).
<— PIE *ḱlewH- “to clean”: Gr κλύζωει 'flow' (cataclysm, клизма), Latin cluō 'to clean', cloāca 'sewer pipe';
3) Bernstein: from a PIE *(s)lawos, cognate to Gr λαός "population, people".
Ok. So it's either speaking ones or Dnipro people. Haven't found which one is the main version.

